<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Characene</h1><p class="st-meta">141 BCE – 222 CE (approx.) · capital Charax Spasinou</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A trading kingdom at the head of the Gulf, centred on Charax Spasinou, that linked Parthian Mesopotamia with India, Arabia and Palmyra.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyspaosines">Hyspaosines</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid</a> governor of the head of the Gulf, refounded the flooded city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charax-spasinou">Charax Spasinou</a> and declared independence around 141 BCE as Seleucid power collapsed. He briefly held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 127 BCE before becoming a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Characene, also called Mesene, prospered from long-distance trade: ships from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the Gulf unloaded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charax-spasinou">Charax</a>, and Palmyrene merchants organised caravans from there to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, as inscriptions at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palmyrene-empire">Palmyra</a> show. Kings minted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> tetradrachms with Greek legends. Its population included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and Iranians, and the geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-charax">Isidore of Charax</a> came from the city. The Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> visited King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attambelos-vii">Attambelos VII</a> in 116 CE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> conquered Characene around 222 CE, and it became the Sasanian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meshan">Meshan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charax-spasinou">Charax</a> declined as rivers shifted, its role later taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 141 BCE – 222 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:56.90%;width:7.22%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>141 BCE – 222 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Charax Spasinou</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hyspaosines, Attambelos VII, Meredates</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesopotamian, Greek and Iranian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Iraq, Kuwait, Iran</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Characene, Mesene, Mesene-Characene</dd></dl></aside></div>
